802.11 is a set of IEEE standards that defines how Wi-Fi wireless networks work. It specifies how devices such as laptops, smartphones, and wireless routers communicate over radio waves without needing an Ethernet cable.

For your CompTIA A+ studies, think of 802.11 as the technical standard behind Wi-Fi.

## Common 802.11 Wi-Fi standards

| Standard | Wi-Fi generation | Frequency band    | Maximum theoretical speed |
| -------- | ---------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------- |
| 802.11a  | —                | 5 GHz             | 54 Mbps                   |
| 802.11b  | —                | 2.4 GHz           | 11 Mbps                   |
| 802.11g  | —                | 2.4 GHz           | 54 Mbps                   |
| 802.11n  | Wi-Fi 4          | 2.4 / 5 GHz       | 600 Mbps                  |
| 802.11ac | Wi-Fi 5          | 5 GHz             | About 6.9 Gbps            |
| 802.11ax | Wi-Fi 6 / 6E     | 2.4 / 5 / 6 GHz\* | About 9.6 Gbps            |
| 802.11be | Wi-Fi 7          | 2.4 / 5 / 6 GHz\* | About 46 Gbps             |

\*The available bands depend on the router, device, and regulatory region. Speeds shown are theoretical maximums, not typical real-world speeds.

